# Large data directories (excluded from GitHub)
# See docs/DATA_FILES_REFERENCE.md for the complete list and instructions.
#
# The corpus SHIPS in this repository (3,484 .tess files, about 477 MB, a
# deliberate choice that file records). A blanket `texts/` rule did not
# untrack any of it -- it only hid the NEXT file: a newly imported text did
# not appear in `git status`, was skipped by `git add texts/`, and was never
# committed unless somebody remembered `git add -f`, while the index build,
# the lemma cache and the deploy that followed all behaved as though it had
# shipped. Corpus import is this project's most frequent change, so the
# languages that ship are visible to git, one directory at a time:
texts/*
!texts/la/
!texts/grc/
!texts/en/
!texts/cop/
!texts/he/
!texts/it/
!texts/gmh/
!texts/fro/
# Languages still in development (Persian, Urdu, Arabic) stay ignored on
# purpose: they are not to reach production, and an ignore rule is a poor
# guard but a useful reminder. Add the directory above when one ships.
#
# Scratch directories inside a language folder (a dated set of files pulled
# out of the corpus, say) are not part of the corpus:
texts/*/_*/
# data/* rather than data/ so single curated files can be un-ignored below
# (git cannot re-include a child of an ignored DIRECTORY).
data/*
data/la_mqdq_temp/
# Small curated data files ARE tracked (the blanket rule above is for the
# multi-GB indexes and tables):
!data/text_descriptions.json
!data/lemma_tables/
!data/text_genres.csv
!data/translation_pairs.json
cache/

# Private/local files
.private/

# Attached assets (user uploads during development)
attached_assets/

# Research artifacts (large files)
research/datasets/
research/references/

# Private drafts and manuals (stay on Replit only)
docs/ARTICLE_METHODS_DRAFT.md
docs/PRIVATE_DEPLOYMENT_MANUAL.md

# Search cache (regenerated at runtime)

# UD treebank source data (large, download separately)
data/treebanks/

# Evaluation benchmark data (large analysis/benchmark JSON files)
evaluation/
# Tests ARE tracked, even though the research notes around them are not. A
# regression test that lives only on one machine cannot stop a regression: the
# quotation-channel test would have caught an eleven-week production outage, and
# it was written into an ignored directory.
!evaluation/scripts/reference_test_arma.py
!tests/
!tests/**

# Pre-computed embeddings: 1,704 files, about 2 GB, ARE committed already
# (docs/DATA_FILES_REFERENCE.md calls that a deliberate choice for ease of
# setup), and they stay tracked -- an ignore rule never untracks anything.
# The directory stays ignored for what comes NEXT, because an embedding run
# writes gigabytes of binary files that can always be recomputed from the
# texts, and 3.6 GB of them appearing in `git status` would bury everything
# else. Committing a new one is therefore a deliberate act: `git add -f`.
# Unlike a text file, nothing is lost if it never reaches git.
backend/embeddings/

# Research directory (private plans, writing, evaluation output)
research/
docs/FUSION_ARCHITECTURE.md
docs/HOW_TESSERAE_SEARCHES.md

